& Accuracy alone does not determine usability; latency and error visibility matter. \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Critical synthesis of related work}
\label{tab:sota_synthesis}
\end{table}
The review reveals a gap that is narrow but practically important. Large meeting-transcription benchmarks now evaluate far-field, overlapped and speaker-attributed speech, while domain-adaptation studies often optimise a single model on large or specialised datasets. Less evidence is available on the marginal value of low-cost interventions inside one lightweight, locally deployable multilingual pipeline. The present work addresses that narrower question: it holds the evaluation set and scorer fixed and measures noise reduction, prompting, forced language identification and checkpoint size under constrained compute. It does \emph{not} claim to benchmark corporate meetings end to end. Instead, it provides pilot evidence on trilingual telephone speech and identifies which components merit validation on a genuine meeting corpus. This narrower positioning preserves the study's contribution without equating MInDS-14 with NOTSOFAR-1 or CHiME-8.
\vspace{0.5em}
In summary, this chapter has established the conceptual foundations of ASR and its evaluation, reviewed the strengths and limitations of existing approaches, and identified the intersection (noise, multilingualism and domain vocabulary in a single deployable CPU pipeline) where evidence is scarce. The next chapter presents the methodological approach designed to produce that evidence.
%=========================== [Chapter 2] =======================
\chapter{Methodological Approach}
This chapter presents the methodological approach adopted to answer the research questions posed in the general introduction. It first describes the step-by-step research process, then presents the proposed system architecture, the construction of the evaluation dataset, and the design of the experimental conditions and hypotheses. Throughout, the points where the implementation diverges from the originally planned design are stated explicitly, so that the results of Chapter III can be interpreted against what was actually built and measured.
\section{Research Process}
The methodological approach rests on a progressive, structured process in six main steps, represented in Figure~\ref{fig:process}:
\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  pstep/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=myColor, fill=myColor!8, text width=3.4cm, minimum height=1.15cm, align=center, font=\small},
  arr/.style={-{Stealth}, thick, myColor}]
\node[pstep] (s1) {1. Literature review};
\node[pstep, right=0.55cm of s1] (s2) {2. Dataset construction (MInDS-14 + noise augmentation)};
\node[pstep, right=0.55cm of s2] (s3) {3. Pipeline implementation (faster-whisper)};
\node[pstep, below=0.7cm of s3] (s4) {4. Definition of conditions and hypotheses};
\node[pstep, left=0.55cm of s4] (s5) {5. Evaluation campaign (180 runs)};
\node[pstep, left=0.55cm of s5] (s6) {6. Analysis and interpretation};
\draw[arr] (s1) -- (s2);
\draw[arr] (s2) -- (s3);
\draw[arr] (s3) -- (s4);
\draw[arr] (s4) -- (s5);
\draw[arr] (s5) -- (s6);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Research process}
\label{fig:process}
\end{figure}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Literature review} (Chapter I), identifying the relevant architectures, robustness and adaptation techniques, and the evaluation metrics of the field;
    \item \textbf{Construction of a ground-truth evaluation dataset}, multilingual (English, French, German) and covering clean and noise-augmented conditions;
    \item \textbf{Implementation of a configurable transcription pipeline} based on \textit{faster-whisper}, exposing noise-reduction preprocessing, domain-vocabulary prompting, and automatic or forced language identification;
    \item \textbf{Definition of the experimental conditions and hypotheses}, fixed in writing \emph{before} any evaluation run, so that results are judged against a pre-specified target rather than adjusted after observing the outcomes;
    \item \textbf{Execution of the evaluation campaign}, 180 transcription runs across five conditions;
    \item \textbf{Analysis and interpretation of the results} against the hypotheses, including where the evidence is inconclusive or negative.
\end{enumerate}
This process links the conception of the solution coherently to its implementation and its experimental evaluation; steps 2 to 5 are detailed in the remainder of this chapter, and step 6 is the subject of Chapter III.
\section{Proposed Approach and Architecture}
\subsection{System Architecture}
The speech-to-text pipeline studied here is one component of a three-topic accessibility platform developed within the Nestl\'e/BIT collaboration: an OCR engine for scanned corporate documents (Topic 1), the STT engine that is the subject of this work (Topic 2), and a retrieval-augmented question-answering layer over both (Topic 3). All three are exposed through a single FastAPI backend running entirely on local, open-source components: \textit{faster-whisper} for transcription \cite{fasterwhisper2023}, Tesseract-based OCR, multilingual sentence embeddings with ChromaDB as vector store, and a locally hosted Ollama model for answer generation. The codebase labels this as a \enquote{Phase~1} configuration, with a planned \enquote{Phase~2} migration to Azure-managed services; this work evaluates the Phase~1, locally hosted STT pipeline, and the Azure migration is discussed in Chapter III strictly as a deployment proposal, not as a system that was built or measured. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} presents the overall architecture.
\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=myColor, fill=myColor!8, text width=2.9cm, minimum height=1.0cm, align=center, font=\small},
  wide/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=myColor, fill=myColor!15, text width=10.6cm, minimum height=0.9cm, align=center, font=\small\bfseries},
  arr/.style={-{Stealth}, thick, myColor}]
\node[wide] (front) {Frontend (upload page / live recording page)};
\node[box, below=0.8cm of front.south west, anchor=north west, xshift=-0.0cm] (api) {FastAPI backend\\ \texttt{POST /transcribe}};
\node[box, right=0.5cm of api] (ws) {WebSocket\\ \texttt{/ws/transcribe}\\ (Silero VAD)};
\node[box, right=0.5cm of ws] (rag) {RAG layer\\ \texttt{POST /ask}\\ (ChromaDB + Ollama)};
\node[box, below=0.8cm of api] (prep) {Preprocessing\\ 16 kHz mono +\\ \texttt{noisereduce}};
\node[box, below=0.8cm of ws] (whisper) {faster-whisper\\ (INT8 on CPU)\\ prompt / language};
\node[box, below=0.8cm of rag] (diar) {pyannote\\ diarization\\ (optional, token-gated)};
\draw[arr] (front.south -| api.north) -- (api.north);
\draw[arr] (front.south -| ws.north) -- (ws.north);
\draw[arr] (front.south -| rag.north) -- (rag.north);
\draw[arr] (api) -- (prep);
\draw[arr] (prep) -- (whisper);
\draw[arr] (ws) -- (whisper);
\draw[arr] (whisper) -- (diar);
\draw[arr, dashed] (whisper.east) to[out=-20,in=-160] (rag.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Overall architecture of the implemented platform (Phase 1, fully local)}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}
The core transcription path is organised around a \texttt{TranscribeConfig} structure centralising the exposed options: model size, target language (or automatic detection), noise-reduction preprocessing, domain-vocabulary prompting, and word-level timestamps. A call to \texttt{transcribe\_file()} optionally resamples the input to 16\,kHz mono and applies spectral-gating noise reduction (\texttt{noisereduce}), before passing the audio to a cached \texttt{WhisperModel} instance running with INT8 quantisation on CPU. When domain prompting is enabled, a fixed string of Nestl\'e-related vocabulary is injected as the decoder's initial prompt. The function returns the transcript, timestamped segments, the detected language, and the processing duration. A separate WebSocket path serves live transcription: incoming audio chunks are classified by a Silero VAD model, buffered until 0.6\,s of silence marks the end of an utterance, then transcribed by the same cached model. Speaker diarization (pyannote \cite{bredin2020pyannote}) is applied by aligning each Whisper segment to the diarization turn with maximal temporal overlap, when a Hugging Face access token is available.
Two divergences between the planned design and the as-built system were identified during the pilot campaign and are reported here explicitly, because they shaped what that campaign could measure; both were subsequently closed in a post-pilot engineering iteration. First, the VAD component, although loaded at startup, was \textbf{never invoked} by the batch transcription path at pilot time; it has since been wired in through faster-whisper's native \texttt{vad\_filter} option (the same Silero model, integrated at inference), enabling the originally planned Condition C to be measured (Chapter III). Second, the language detected by Whisper per utterance was not persisted in the evaluation logs, so the pilot's per-run detection results had to be recovered indirectly by comparing hypothesis texts between automatic-detection and forced-language runs of the same file; the harness now records a \texttt{detected\_language} column for all subsequent runs. The live WebSocket path, which at pilot time hardcoded French as decoding language and its own inline prompt, has likewise been parameterised: the language is now a connection parameter (\texttt{?lang=fr|en|de|auto}, defaulting to automatic detection), and the inline prompt was removed after prompt-leakage hallucinations were observed on silent segments, consistent with the prompt-injection finding of Chapter III. Live and batch transcription remain architecturally distinct, and only the batch path was evaluated in the pilot.
\subsection{Dataset Construction}
Evaluating the pipeline requires audio with known ground-truth transcripts, spanning several languages and at least two noise conditions. No Nestl\'e-internal meeting recording could be used: the project's data policy prohibits committing confidential audio to the evaluation set. The public corpora initially recommended (Common Voice, LibriSpeech, FLEURS, VoxPopuli) were attempted first and proved impractical in the project's sandboxed environment (interrupted streaming downloads, multi-hundred-megabyte shards without row-level indexing). This constraint is reported rather than omitted, since it explains why the dataset finally used is not one of the originally recommended corpora.
The dataset actually used is \textbf{PolyAI/MInDS-14} \cite{gerz2021multilingual}, a CC-BY-4.0-licensed corpus of real, spontaneous telephone-banking speech, from which English, French and German subsets were sampled. Twenty-four clean clips were drawn directly from the corpus (eight per language) with their genuine reference transcripts. Twelve noisy variants (four per language) were generated by adding synthetic white noise at a controlled 8\,dB signal-to-noise ratio to twelve of those clean recordings, reusing the same ground-truth transcript; additive white Gaussian noise at a controlled SNR is a standard, well-characterised way to degrade speech intelligibility in a reproducible manner \cite{graetzer2021intelligibility}. The resulting dataset contains 36 audio files but only 24 distinct utterances. Consequently, the causal effect of added noise must be estimated on the twelve matched clean/noisy pairs; comparing all 24 clean files with the 12 noisy files is retained only as a descriptive split because the two groups differ in utterance composition. Table~\ref{tab:dataset} summarises the files available to each condition.
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Language} & \textbf{Clean} & \textbf{Noisy} & \textbf{Total} \\ \hline
English (en) & 8 & 4 & 12 \\ \hline
French (fr) & 8 & 4 & 12 \\ \hline
German (de) & 8 & 4 & 12 \\ \hline
\textbf{Total} & \textbf{24} & \textbf{12} & \textbf{36} \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Evaluation samples by language and noise condition}
\label{tab:dataset}
\end{table}
Two limitations follow directly from this substitution and are treated as first-class limitations rather than footnotes. First, the speech is single-speaker, 8\,kHz telephone audio rather than multi-speaker, full-bandwidth meeting audio, so it exercises neither diarization nor the full acoustic variability of a meeting room. Second, it contains none of the Nestl\'e-specific vocabulary that domain prompting is designed to help with, which directly limits what that condition's results can demonstrate, a constraint that Chapter III's analysis takes into account explicitly.
\subsection{Statistical Analysis Plan}
All headline intervention effects are computed at the sample level. Comparisons between two systems evaluated on the same utterances use paired differences and a paired non-parametric bootstrap: the 24 independent source utterances are sampled as clusters with replacement 10{,}000 times using seed 42; each clean/noisy derivative remains inside its source cluster, and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles define the 95\,\% confidence interval. The clean-to-noisy degradation uses the same procedure on the twelve utterances for which both versions exist. Descriptive means over all 24 clean files remain useful for characterising the full evaluation set, but they are not interpreted as a matched noise effect. With only 12 noise pairs and 24 independent source utterances for system comparisons, confidence intervals and per-language results are treated as pilot evidence; statistical non-significance is not interpreted as proof of equivalence.

This distinction yields three primary uncertainty estimates used in Chapter III. The matched baseline noise penalty is 35.3 WER points, with a 95\,\% paired-bootstrap interval of [7.0, 60.0]. The reduction from Condition B (\texttt{tiny}) to Condition I (\texttt{large-v3}) is 37.6 points, with interval [24.3, 50.7]. The paired LoRA effect (J0 minus J) is 1.0 point, with interval [-2.4, 5.3], which spans zero. These intervals quantify sampling uncertainty over the observed utterances; they do not cover corpus-selection, hardware or domain-shift uncertainty.
\subsection{Experimental Conditions and Hypotheses}
Five experimental conditions were run, each applied to all 36 samples, for a total of \textbf{180 transcription runs} (Table~\ref{tab:conditions}).
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Preprocessing} & \textbf{Domain prompt} & \textbf{Language} \\ \hline
A -- Baseline & tiny & off & off & auto-detect \\ \hline
B -- Noise reduction & tiny & on & off & auto-detect \\ \hline
D -- Domain prompt & tiny & on & on & auto-detect \\ \hline
E -- Forced language & tiny & on & on & true language \\ \hline
F -- Base model & base & on & off & auto-detect \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Experimental conditions ($n=36$ samples per condition, 180 runs in total)}
\label{tab:conditions}
\end{table}
Conditions A through E form a deliberate \textbf{cumulative chain}: B adds noise reduction to A, D adds domain prompting to B, and E adds forced language to D, so that comparing any adjacent pair isolates the marginal effect of exactly one intervention while holding everything upstream fixed. Condition F matches B exactly except for substituting the \texttt{base} model for \texttt{tiny}, making F~vs.~B the cleanest comparison for isolating model size. This chained design is what allows Chapter III to attribute each observed change in WER, CER or RTF to a single named methodological choice rather than to an unspecified combination of changes.
A sixth condition originally planned (Condition C, Silero VAD preprocessing) was \textbf{not run during the pilot}: as reported above, the VAD component was defined in the codebase but never invoked by the evaluated transcription path, and its absence was recorded as a methodological finding rather than silently dropped. After the pilot, VAD was wired into the batch path via faster-whisper's native Silero integration, and Condition C (configured identically to B plus VAD) was completed as a 36-run GPU extension, reported in Chapter III. Similarly, Condition F was originally specified as a \texttt{small}/\texttt{medium}/\texttt{large-v2} comparison and was run as \texttt{tiny}/\texttt{base} instead, for the disclosed hardware reason (CPU-only environment); the comparison's intent (model size versus accuracy and speed) is preserved with feasible checkpoint sizes, and the full ladder (\texttt{small}, \texttt{medium}, \texttt{large-v3}) was subsequently completed as a GPU extension study, together with a paired LoRA fine-tuning study addressing the adaptation question directly, both reported in Chapter III.
Four hypotheses were formulated \emph{before} running the evaluation, corresponding to the research objectives, and are stated as originally framed so that Chapter III reports against them rather than reverse-engineering hypotheses from favourable results:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{H1:} noise-reduction preprocessing reduces WER on noisy audio without materially increasing WER on clean audio;
    \item \textbf{H2:} domain-vocabulary prompting reduces errors specifically on named entities, with negligible effect on general accuracy;
    \item \textbf{H3:} the CPU-only pipeline achieves a real-time factor below 1.0 across the tested model sizes;
    \item \textbf{H4:} forcing the correct language outperforms automatic detection, particularly on short or noisy clips.
\end{itemize}
Metrics (WER, CER, RTF) are computed by a self-contained, pure-Python Levenshtein implementation with fixed, auditable normalisation (lower-casing, punctuation stripping, whitespace collapsing), rather than an opaque third-party scorer. Every run appends one row to a results file carrying the full reference and hypothesis text alongside its metrics, so any reported number can be traced back to the exact text pair that produced it and independently re-scored; decoding is deterministic given fixed weights and settings, and dependency versions are pinned. All 180 runs completed without a runtime error or missing metric value. The experimental plan was fixed internally in writing before evaluation; because it was not deposited in a public time-stamped registry, this thesis uses the term \emph{pre-specified}, not \emph{pre-registered}.
\vspace{0.5em}
In summary, this chapter has presented a six-step research process, a fully local transcription architecture whose as-built divergences from the planned design are documented rather than hidden, a 36-sample trilingual evaluation dataset with controlled noise augmentation, and a pre-specified experimental design of five chained conditions and four hypotheses. The next chapter presents the implementation environment, the execution of this evaluation campaign, and the analysis of its results.
%=========================== [Chapter 3] =======================
\chapter{Implementation, Experiments and Results}
This chapter presents the implementation environment (hardware and software), the justification of the technological choices, the realisation process of the evaluation campaign, and the results obtained. The 180-run pilot matrix defined in Chapter II is executed, then extended on GPU with a model-size ladder, the subsequently completed VAD condition, a paired LoRA fine-tuning study, a wav2vec 2.0 and Conformer cross-architecture benchmark (468 transcription runs in total), and a diarization assessment on seven simulated meetings, analysed research question by research question, and discussed against the pre-specified hypotheses and the state of the art.
\section{Implementation Environment}
\subsection{Hardware Environment}
Development and the canonical evaluation campaign were carried out in a CPU-only sandboxed environment, using faster-whisper's INT8-quantised CPU code path (Table~\ref{tab:hardware}). This configuration was a deliberate framing as much as a constraint: it characterises what an organisation can deploy on standard office hardware, without dedicated GPU servers, and the reported real-time factors are therefore a conservative lower bound on production throughput. A complementary run of the identical matrix was subsequently executed on a CUDA-enabled GPU machine in float16 precision, to measure how the speed side of the trade-off shifts under acceleration (Section 3.3.1).
