Two observations shape the analysis that follows. First, absolute WER is high (48--63\,\%) by the standards of headline Whisper benchmarks. This is expected rather than alarming: the dataset is real 8\,kHz telephone-quality spontaneous speech rather than studio-quality read speech, the models tested are the two smallest checkpoints, and a third of the samples are deliberately noise-degraded. Manual inspection of individual transcripts (\enquote{bank} recognised as \enquote{bake}, German \enquote{einzuzahlen} as \enquote{einzuzeilen}) confirms genuine acoustic-confusion errors rather than pipeline failures, consistent with zero runs producing an error. Second, the largest single effect in the whole campaign is model size (Condition F), ahead of every preprocessing or prompting intervention.
\subsubsection*{Extension: the Full Model-Size Ladder (GPU)}
To complete the accuracy/speed curve begun by the tiny/base comparison, the evaluation matrix was extended with three further conditions (G: \texttt{small}, H: \texttt{medium}, I: \texttt{large-v3}), identical in configuration to Condition B except for model size, executed on an NVIDIA RTX 4060 Laptop GPU (8\,GB VRAM) in float16, 108 additional runs, bringing the campaign to 288 runs in total. Table~\ref{tab:ladder} reports the resulting ladder; for a fair speed comparison, the RTF column reports GPU-float16 values for \emph{all} sizes (tiny and base re-measured on the same GPU), since the canonical CPU RTFs of Table~\ref{tab:results_agg} are not comparable across backends.
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Mean WER} & \textbf{Mean CER} & \textbf{Clean WER} & \textbf{Noisy WER} & \textbf{RTF (GPU)} \\ \hline
tiny (B) & 39M & 62.2\,\% & 44.6\,\% & 49.2\,\% & 88.1\,\% & 0.144 \\ \hline
base (F) & 74M & 48.3\,\% & 33.6\,\% & 32.9\,\% & 79.2\,\% & 0.188 \\ \hline
small (G) & 244M & 33.3\,\% & 24.6\,\% & 21.6\,\% & 56.7\,\% & 0.146 \\ \hline
medium (H) & 769M & 25.9\,\% & 21.1\,\% & 17.3\,\% & 43.3\,\% & 0.195 \\ \hline
large-v3 (I) & 1.55B & \textbf{24.6\,\%} & \textbf{20.5\,\%} & \textbf{16.6\,\%} & \textbf{40.7\,\%} & 0.359 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Model-size ladder ($n=36$ per size; G/H/I and the RTF column measured on GPU, float16; tiny/base WER/CER are the canonical CPU figures)}
\label{tab:ladder}
\end{table}
Three results stand out. First, the WER curve is \textbf{strictly monotone} across all five sizes (62.2 $\to$ 48.3 $\to$ 33.3 $\to$ 25.9 $\to$ 24.6\,\%), with clearly diminishing returns after \texttt{medium}: the last $21\times$ increase in parameters (74M $\to$ 1.55B) buys 23.7 points, but the final step (\texttt{medium} $\to$ \texttt{large-v3}) only 1.3. Second, model size does more against noise than any explicit mitigation tested: noisy-audio WER falls from 88.1\,\% (tiny) to 40.7\,\% (large-v3), dwarfing the 8.3-point gain of noise-reduction preprocessing. Third, the language-identification pathology of Section 3.3.2.3 largely disappears at scale: French WER collapses from 64.1\,\% (base) to 21.8\,\% (large-v3) and German reaches 8.3\,\%, while English becomes the \emph{worst} language at larger sizes (43.8\,\%), driven by a small number of persistently difficult clips, a reminder that per-language aggregates over twelve clips remain sample-sensitive. On speed, \texttt{small}'s RTF ($0.146$) is essentially equal to \texttt{tiny}'s ($0.144$) on GPU despite $6\times$ more parameters: more accurate models terminate decoding earlier on difficult audio instead of extending long hallucinated hypotheses, partially offsetting their higher per-step cost. Two caveats bound the ladder: the G/H/I accuracy figures were produced under float16 on GPU, whereas tiny/base canonical figures are INT8-CPU (the cross-backend divergence measured at those sizes was small relative to the 14--24-point model-size effects); and no CPU RTF was measured for \texttt{small} and above, so whether they remain below real time \emph{without} a GPU is explicitly not claimed here. In deployment terms, \texttt{small} emerges as the best accuracy-per-cost point on this dataset, and \texttt{medium} as the accuracy sweet spot when a GPU is available.
\subsection{Analysis of Results}
\subsubsection{Noise and Noise Reduction (H1)}
Background noise is a major driver of error, but its effect must be estimated on matched content. Across the twelve utterances for which both versions exist, baseline WER rises from 61.1\,\% before augmentation to 96.4\,\% after augmentation, a paired increase of 35.3 points (95\,\% paired-bootstrap CI [7.0, 60.0]). The wider descriptive split, 47.0\,\% over all 24 clean files versus 96.4\,\% over the 12 noisy files, is not used as the causal estimate because the additional clean utterances are easier on average. The matched result nevertheless supports the substantive conclusion that noise is one of the largest factors observed.

Comparing B against A on the same files isolates the effect of spectral-gating preprocessing: on noisy audio, WER falls from 96.4\,\% to 88.1\,\% ($-8.3$ points); over the full clean set it \emph{rises} from 47.0\,\% to 49.2\,\% ($+2.2$ points). This is a partial confirmation of H1: noise reduction helps where it is needed but is not free on clean signal. Spectral gating is signal-blind and may attenuate weak speech components together with noise. Because the sample is small and contains only stationary white-noise augmentation, this mechanism remains an interpretation rather than a directly measured phonetic explanation. For a deployment facing unknown acoustics, conditional preprocessing or an enhancement model validated on non-stationary meeting noise is preferable to enabling spectral gating blindly.
\subsubsection{Domain-Vocabulary Prompting (H2)}
Comparing D against B isolates the fixed Nestl\'e-vocabulary prompt. The aggregate effect is small (62.2\,\% to 61.3\,\% WER) with CER moving slightly in the opposite direction. This must be read as a \textbf{null result for a specific, disclosed reason}: the MInDS-14 dataset contains none of the Nestl\'e vocabulary the prompt targets, so Condition D can only measure the \emph{cost} of prompting when the prompted vocabulary is absent, not its benefit when present. The campaign did, however, capture the cost side concretely: on one noisy German clip, the model hallucinated the prompted text directly into its output, producing \enquote{...it's Nestl\'e Ghana, the center of the conference room...} in a transcript unrelated to Nestl\'e. This \emph{prompt leakage} occurs when a low-confidence decoding pass (noisy audio plus automatic language detection) falls back on the injected context rather than the acoustic content. Static prompt injection is therefore a double-edged mitigation, consistent with the entity-benchmark caution of Chapter I \cite{durmus2026contextual}.
\subsubsection{Automatic Language Identification (H4)}
Comparing E against D isolates language identification. The aggregate gain is modest (1.7 WER points, 4.1 CER points), but hides the strongest finding of the campaign. Direct comparison of hypothesis texts between automatic and forced runs shows a sharp mismatch gradient: English 0/12 (0\,\%), German 2/12 ($\approx$17\,\%), French 5/12 ($\approx$42\,\%). Several French failures are catastrophic at script level: the reference \enquote{bonjour je voudrais changer d'adresse} was auto-detected and transcribed as Cyrillic text with no relation to the spoken content; other short noisy French clips came out in Hebrew, Turkish or Chinese script, a wrong-language output behaviour independently documented as a Whisper failure mode by \cite{koenecke2024careless}. Forcing \texttt{fr} recovered French-script output in every such case. Notably, this recovery barely moves aggregate French WER (74.8\,\% in both D and E): word-level WER scores a wrong-script hallucination and a same-script-but-inexact transcript similarly badly, even though the latter is qualitatively far more usable for a human reader. Both the quantitative and the qualitative result are therefore reported. H4 is \textbf{strongly supported}: in a genuinely multilingual deployment, automatic detection should not be relied on for short or noisy utterances, and forcing the expected language per speaker, channel or meeting is a zero-cost, immediately deployable mitigation.
\subsubsection{Model Size and Real-Time Factor (H3)}
Comparing F against B (identical configuration, \texttt{base} vs.\ \texttt{tiny}) isolates model size. Mean WER falls from 62.2\,\% to 48.3\,\% ($-13.9$ points) and CER from 44.6\,\% to 33.6\,\%, consistently across both noise conditions and all three languages. Mean RTF rises from 0.372 to 0.635 (about $1.9\times$ slower), remaining below 1.0 in aggregate. Across the campaign, however, only 169 of 180 runs (93.9\,\%) stayed below RTF 1.0; the worst case reached 2.877. H3 is therefore \textbf{supported for mean throughput but not for every utterance}. The extended ladder confirms the accuracy trend through \texttt{small}, \texttt{medium} and \texttt{large-v3}; because larger sizes were timed on GPU rather than CPU, it does not extend H3's CPU claim beyond \texttt{base}.
\subsubsection{CPU versus GPU}
Re-executing the identical matrix on a CUDA GPU in float16 confirmed the throughput headroom: RTF improved uniformly (tiny: 0.323 to 0.143, $2.3\times$; base: 0.635 to 0.188, $3.4\times$), with the larger gain on the heavier model. Two caveats bound this comparison: single runs per pair, and (more fundamentally) switching from INT8 to float16 changes the numerical representation of the weights, which propagated through the autoregressive decoder and caused roughly 52 of the 180 transcriptions to differ slightly between backends. Determinism therefore holds \emph{within} a fixed (hardware, compute-type) pairing, not across them; the WER/CER figures reported above remain the canonical CPU ones.
\subsubsection{Condition C Completed: VAD Preprocessing}
After the VAD gap reported in Chapter II was closed, the originally planned Condition C (configuration of B plus Silero VAD, via faster-whisper's native \texttt{vad\_filter}) was run on all 36 samples as a GPU extension. Since these runs execute in float16 on GPU, the honest reference is Condition B \emph{re-measured on the same backend}, not the canonical CPU figures: C achieves a mean WER of 62.2\,\% against 62.7\,\% for B-on-GPU, CER 45.6\,\% against 46.4\,\%, and RTF 0.130 against 0.144, a modest, consistent improvement on all three aggregates. The noise split, however, reveals a genuine trade-off hidden by the aggregate: on clean audio, VAD \emph{improves} WER from 51.5\,\% to 47.7\,\% ($-3.8$ points, consistent with its intended role of discarding non-speech segments before they can be hallucinated into text), but on noisy audio it \emph{degrades} WER from 85.0\,\% to 91.0\,\% ($+6.0$ points), plausibly because speech submerged in noise is misclassified as non-speech and silently dropped before transcription. This is the mirror image of the noise-reduction trade-off measured for H1: each preprocessing technique helps in one regime and costs in the other, which reinforces the chapter's broader conclusion that no single preprocessing switch is safe to enable blindly across unknown acoustic conditions. With twelve noisy clips, this split is directional rather than statistically established, and is reported with that caveat.
\subsubsection{Live-Path Latency}
To address the real-time dimension of the research problem, the live WebSocket path was instrumented to log, for every utterance, the interval between the VAD silence-threshold trigger and the server-side measurement immediately before WebSocket transmission. Browser receipt and rendering are not included. An informal two-session test produced 22 observations from one speaker using mixed languages on an RTX 4060 GPU. The first utterance of each session was defined as cold start and excluded from the steady-state summary. Across the remaining 20 utterances, post-endpointing latency has a \textbf{median of 0.555\,s}, a linearly interpolated \textbf{95th percentile of 0.84\,s}, and a \textbf{maximum of 1.18\,s}. The two cold starts were 2.60 and 3.61\,s. These statistics correct the distinction between p95 and maximum and make the exclusion rule explicit.

This is processing latency after the end-of-speech decision, not complete caption latency. User-perceived delay additionally includes the fixed 0.6\,s silence window, so the median observed delay from final speech sample to delivered transcript is approximately 1.16\,s. No accuracy/latency frontier can be inferred because there is no ground-truth transcript and no alternative endpointing configuration was benchmarked. RQ3 is therefore \emph{partially answered}: the prototype demonstrates steady-state sub-second processing after endpointing, but it does not establish optimal streaming latency without loss of accuracy.
\subsubsection{RQ2 Completed: LoRA Fine-Tuning versus Prompting}
The adaptation question left open by the pilot (prompting was measurable only on its cost side) was addressed directly in a final post-pilot extension: parameter-efficient LoRA fine-tuning of \texttt{whisper-small}. A 300-clip training split (100 per language) was drawn from MInDS-14 with a documented non-contamination procedure: every candidate whose normalised transcript matched any of the evaluation references was excluded (8 exclusions in English, 12 in French, the excess revealing transcript duplicates in the corpus itself, and 8 in German), selection was randomised under a fixed seed, and a final assertion verified that no training transcript overlaps the evaluation set. Training used LoRA rank 16 on the attention projections (1.77M trainable parameters, 0.73\,\% of the model), three epochs in float16 on the RTX 4060; the loss fell from 4.71 to 1.95 in just over two minutes of compute.
Evaluation was deliberately \emph{paired}: the fine-tuned model and the unmodified \texttt{whisper-small} baseline were both run on the 36 canonical samples through an identical direct HuggingFace inference path with the same scorer, so that only the weights differ between the two passes. The baseline (J0) scores 31.2\,\% WER / 24.2\,\% CER; the fine-tuned model (J) scores 30.2\,\% / 23.0\,\%, a 1.0-point WER improvement. The 95\,\% paired-bootstrap interval for J0 minus J is [-2.4, 5.3] points and spans zero. Per-file analysis likewise shows a sparse and unstable pattern (7 improved, 6 degraded, 23 unchanged). The study therefore provides no evidence of a reliable LoRA benefit on this matched-domain sample; it also cannot establish equivalence. The gain concentrates descriptively on clean audio and on German and English, while French is unchanged. Unlike prompting, fine-tuning exhibited no observed prompt-leakage side-effect.
Two conclusions follow for RQ2. First, on this dataset \textbf{both low-cost adaptation strategies land in the same place}: decode-time prompting shifted aggregate WER by $\approx$1 point (D vs.\ B) and in-domain LoRA fine-tuning by $\approx$1 point (J vs.\ J0), each an order of magnitude below the model-size effect (14--38 points) and below the qualitative effect of forced language identification. The likely reason is disclosed rather than speculated away: the training split comes from the \emph{same} telephone-banking domain that Whisper's pretraining already covers adequately, so there was little domain gap left to close; the expected value of fine-tuning lies in genuinely divergent target domains (organisation-specific vocabulary above all), which the data constraint of this work prevented testing. Second, an incidental but instructive finding: the vanilla \texttt{whisper-small} scores 31.2\,\% through the HuggingFace/PyTorch path (no preprocessing) versus 33.3\,\% through faster-whisper/CTranslate2 with noise reduction (Condition G), a 2.1-point gap between \emph{nominally identical weights}, comparable in magnitude to the fine-tuning effect itself. This reinforces the backend-constant comparison discipline applied throughout this chapter: implementation and preprocessing choices can matter as much as the adaptation being studied.
\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture Comparison: wav2vec 2.0 (MMS)}
The proposal's third named architecture family was benchmarked in a final extension: \texttt{facebook/mms-1b-all}, Meta's massively multilingual CTC model built on the wav2vec 2.0 encoder, run on the same 36 samples with the same scorer (whose normalisation, lower-casing and punctuation stripping applied to both reference and hypothesis, makes the comparison fair to a CTC model that natively outputs neither casing nor punctuation). MMS scores 35.4\,\% mean WER against 31.2\,\% for the paired \texttt{whisper-small} baseline (J0), with a slightly \emph{better} CER (23.6\,\% vs.\ 24.2\,\%) and near-parity on noisy audio (51.4\,\% vs.\ 51.7\,\%); its CTC decoding is also an order of magnitude faster (RTF 0.028). The per-language pattern is the interesting part: MMS collapses on English telephone speech (60.0\,\%) but beats Whisper-auto on French (25.4\,\% vs.\ 31.0\,\%) and German (20.7\,\% vs.\ 18.6\,\%, near-parity). One confound must be stated: MMS receives the language \emph{by construction} (one adapter is loaded per language) which is architecturally equivalent to forced language identification, whereas J0 ran in automatic detection. Part of MMS's French advantage is therefore this work's own H4 finding resurfacing in another architecture, rather than a pure encoder-quality difference. Overall, Whisper remains the better fit for this use case (native multilingual detection, punctuation, and timestamps) but MMS's noise-parity and speed make it a credible CTC alternative where the language is known in advance.
\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture Comparison: Conformer (NeMo CTC)}
The proposal's third named architecture family was benchmarked to complete the mandatory baseline set: a Conformer, evaluated as NVIDIA NeMo's language-specific \texttt{stt\_\{en,fr,de\}\_conformer\_ctc\_large} checkpoints (CTC decoding), run on the same 36 samples with the same scorer. Conformer scores 26.3\,\% mean WER against 31.2\,\% for the paired \texttt{whisper-small} baseline (J0) and 35.4\,\% for MMS, with a mean CER of 19.9\,\% and a median RTF of 0.014 (again an order of magnitude faster than Whisper, consistent with CTC decoding). The per-language pattern echoes the size ladder: German is strongest (15.3\,\%), French intermediate (23.6\,\%) and English the hardest (39.8\,\%), the last driven by a domain gap, since the English checkpoint is trained on LibriSpeech read speech far from MInDS-14 banking telephony. Noise remains dominant here too: a clean WER of 17.5\,\% degrades to 43.7\,\% on noisy audio ($+26.2$ points), a steeper noise penalty than Whisper shows, unsurprising given these checkpoints were trained on clean read and parliamentary speech with no telephony noise.
Two confounds must be stated, and they are the same discipline applied to MMS. First, the Conformer receives the language \emph{by construction} (one monolingual checkpoint per language), which is architecturally equivalent to forced language identification; part of its apparent margin over \texttt{whisper-small} auto-detection is therefore not a pure architecture effect. The cleaner comparison is Conformer versus MMS, both CTC and language-given. On the same 36 files, MMS minus Conformer equals 9.1 WER points, with a 95\,\% paired-bootstrap interval of [3.0, 16.3]; Conformer is therefore lower on this particular sample. This remains a checkpoint-and-training-data comparison, not a universal proof of Conformer superiority. Second, MInDS-14 is 8\,kHz telephony upsampled to the 16\,kHz these wideband-trained models expect, which cannot restore the high-frequency information absent from the original recording. The practical conclusion is conditional: CTC checkpoints are fast and competitive when language is known, while Whisper offers native multilingual detection, punctuation and timestamps.
\subsubsection{External Validity: Clean Read Speech (FLEURS)}
As a cross-corpus check, the pipeline was additionally run on a corpus of a different nature: FLEURS (Google, CC-BY-4.0), clean, wideband, read speech, twenty clips per language (English, French, German). This set lies \emph{outside} the canonical 468-run campaign and its figures are never merged into the MInDS-14 tables; it serves only to test whether the difficulty measured on MInDS-14 is intrinsic to the pipeline or to the input. On clean read speech, \texttt{large-v3} reaches a mean WER of 7.4\,\% (median 3.4\,\%), against 24.6\,\% on MInDS-14, which indicates that the MInDS-14 difficulty stems from its 8\,kHz telephony, added noise and spontaneous speech rather than from a pipeline defect. One honest caveat bounds this check: on the longer FLEURS utterances the smaller checkpoints (\texttt{tiny} to \texttt{medium}) frequently enter a repetition loop (for example a twelve-word reference decoded as the token \enquote{und} repeated several hundred times), a documented long-form failure mode of Whisper's autoregressive decoder that its official decoding heuristics are designed to suppress \cite{radford2023robust,koenecke2024careless}. Because the evaluation harness keeps a plain deterministic decoding path identical to the MInDS-14 campaign for comparability, those loops are not suppressed; the FLEURS results for \texttt{tiny} to \texttt{medium} are therefore treated as measurements of that failure mode, not of transcription quality, and are excluded, with rare residual loops observed even at \texttt{large-v3}.
\subsubsection{Speaker Diarization on Simulated Meetings}
The diarization component, implemented since the pilot but never exercised on multi-speaker audio, was finally tested end-to-end on seven simulated meetings built by sequentially concatenating MInDS-14 clips (three 2-speaker meetings per language in clean condition, three noisy variants, and one trilingual 3-speaker meeting), with exact ground-truth timings recorded at construction. The pyannote 3.1 pipeline ran successfully on all seven; the mean Diarization Error Rate was 0.585, decomposed into missed speech (33--46\,\%), speaker confusion (12--38\,\%), and, notably, zero false alarms.
These DER figures are reported for completeness but must be read as a measurement of the \textbf{testbed's artificiality more than of pyannote's quality}, for three verifiable reasons. First, the ground truth marks each clip's full span as speech, including its internal pauses, so pyannote's tighter speech regions register as \enquote{missed speech} by construction. Second, the apparent over-segmentation (3--7 hypothesised speakers against 2--3 labelled ones) is largely pyannote being \emph{right}: the concatenated MInDS-14 clips genuinely come from different real speakers, and the SPEAKER\_A/B labelling is the fiction. Third, the giveaway: noisy meetings score \emph{better} DER than clean ones (0.505 vs.\ 0.644), because noise masks inter-speaker voice differences and pushes pyannote toward fewer clusters that accidentally match the artificial labels; a genuine diarization improvement from added noise is not plausible. The honest conclusion is architectural rather than metric: the diarization path works mechanically end-to-end, cross-talk remains untested (turns are sequential by construction), and a valid accuracy assessment requires real multi-speaker recordings with per-speaker continuity, exactly the confidential meeting data whose absence bounds this work throughout.
\subsection{Discussion and Comparison with the State of the Art}
\textbf{Hypotheses revisited.} H1 is partially supported: preprocessing improves the noisy variants but harms the full clean set. H2 remains unanswered on its intended benefit side because no evaluation utterance contains the target Nestl\'e vocabulary; only its near-neutral aggregate cost and one prompt-leakage failure were observed. H3 is supported only at the aggregate level for \texttt{tiny} and \texttt{base}: their mean RTFs are below 1, but 11 of 180 individual CPU runs exceed real time. H4 receives the strongest qualitative support, although the missing pilot-time language labels make the 42\,\% French figure an inference from paired outputs rather than a direct LID accuracy measurement. These qualified verdicts are reported against hypotheses fixed in writing before evaluation.

\textbf{Research-question coverage.} RQ1 is partially answered by a controlled comparison of Whisper sizes, MMS and Conformer under clean and synthetic-noise conditions; generalisation to corporate meetings remains untested. RQ2 is partially answered because prompting and LoRA were implemented and compared, but neither was evaluated on genuine workplace vocabulary or domain-shifted meeting speech. RQ3 is partially answered by RTF and an instrumented WebSocket prototype, but the study does not optimise an accuracy/latency frontier or evaluate true frame-synchronous streaming. This distinction between completed engineering tasks and answered scientific questions is central to the interpretation of the contribution.

\textbf{Positioning against published benchmarks.} The absolute WER levels reported here are far above Whisper's headline figures \cite{radford2023robust}, and the gap is informative about 8\,kHz spontaneous telephony, small checkpoints and synthetic noise. It should not be described as a corporate-meeting benchmark. NOTSOFAR-1 and CHiME-8 provide the appropriate external frame for far-field, overlapped and speaker-attributed meetings \cite{vinnikov2024notsofar,cornell2024chime8}; this thesis instead contributes a constrained component-level ablation with a common scorer. The model-size gain is the most stable effect (37.6 points from B to I; paired-bootstrap 95\,\% interval [24.3, 50.7]), while prompting and LoRA remain uncertain at this sample size.
\textbf{Integration and deployment.} The deployment context matters for positioning this work: Nestl\'e's collaboration stack is built on Microsoft Teams, complemented by Microsoft 365 Copilot and by NesGPT, an internal, security-vetted generative-AI assistant used by tens of thousands of employees \cite{worklife2024nesgpt,arec2023nestle}. Generic cloud transcription (such as Copilot's meeting notes) does exist in that stack, but it is neither accessibility-first (it offers no personalised layer for hearing-impaired users, and no measured guarantees on code-switched or brand-vocabulary speech) nor auditable, although its output accuracy can still be measured against reference transcripts; no head-to-head evaluation of that service was performed here. The very existence of NesGPT, created because Nestl\'e requires generative AI to run under internal security control, supports the design premise of this work's Phase 1: an accessibility pipeline whose audio, transcripts and embeddings never leave the local environment, and whose error behaviour is measured rather than assumed. Within the broader platform, every completed transcription is chunked, embedded with a multilingual model and stored in ChromaDB, so that a hearing-impaired employee can ask a natural-language question and receive an answer grounded in the actual meeting transcript, strategically more important for accessibility than raw transcription alone. The retrieval and answer-generation quality of that layer belongs to Topic 3 and was not evaluated here. The codebase documents a Phase 2 migration path toward Azure-managed services (Azure AI Speech, Document Intelligence, AI Search, Azure OpenAI); only one path has scaffolded code, which deliberately fails loudly (\texttt{NotImplementedError}) rather than degrading silently, and no Azure service was provisioned or measured in this work; the migration is recorded as a documented intention, not a result. Finally, the current pilot has no authentication or per-user scoping on its endpoints: any client able to reach the question-answering endpoint can retrieve content from any ingested transcript. These are known, explicitly out-of-scope gaps for a pilot-stage system, stated here because they bear directly on real-user deployment.
\vspace{0.5em}
In summary, this chapter has presented a fully local implementation environment and its justification, a traceable and resumable evaluation process, and the results of a 180-run campaign: noise is the dominant error factor, noise reduction is a real trade-off, domain prompting could only be measured on its cost side, forced language identification is the clearest actionable win, and model size is the largest single accuracy lever at bounded computational cost. The general conclusion gathers these findings and the perspectives they open.
%=========================== [General Conclusion] =======================
\chapter*{General Conclusion and Perspectives}
\thispagestyle{fancy}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{General Conclusion and Perspectives}
\section*{Summary of the Work}
This work set out to measure, rather than assume, how a local, open-source Whisper-based pipeline performs on noisy multilingual speech and how far low-cost mitigations close the resulting gap. The engineering objectives were achieved: a licensed trilingual evaluation set was assembled (SO1), a configurable \textit{faster-whisper} pipeline was implemented (SO2), and a pre-specified 180-run CPU campaign plus 288 extension runs were completed without recorded runtime errors (SO3). The broader scientific objective is only partially met because the available corpus represents single-speaker banking telephony rather than corporate meetings. The contribution is therefore a reproducible pilot and component analysis, not a deployment validation.
\section*{Main Results}
Four measured findings stand out. First, matched noise augmentation raises baseline WER from 61.1\,\% to 96.4\,\% on twelve paired utterances, a 35.3-point increase with a wide but positive paired-bootstrap interval. Second, spectral-gating preprocessing is conditional rather than universally beneficial: $-8.3$ points on the noisy variants and $+2.2$ points over the full clean set; VAD shows a similarly regime-dependent pattern. Third, model size is the clearest accuracy lever: mean WER falls from 63.4\,\% with \texttt{tiny} to 24.6\,\% with \texttt{large-v3}, and the paired size effect is much larger than sampling uncertainty. Fourth, forcing the expected language repairs several wrong-script outputs, although direct LID accuracy was not logged in the CPU pilot. Prompting and LoRA each shift mean WER by approximately one point; the LoRA interval [-2.4, 5.3] spans zero, while prompting cannot be evaluated on its intended Nestl\'e vocabulary. These are pilot findings, not evidence that the resulting WER is adequate for accessibility-critical deployment.
\section*{Perspectives}
Several concrete extensions follow directly from the limitations. The first scientific priority is evaluation on genuine meeting audio: NOTSOFAR-1, CHiME-8 or a small licensed internal corpus would introduce far-field acoustics, overlap, persistent speakers and speaker-aware metrics such as tcpWER \cite{vinnikov2024notsofar,cornell2024chime8}. The second is a vocabulary-focused set containing organisation and product names, scored with entity recall or entity error rate in addition to WER; contextual-biasing systems such as CB-Whisper provide a stronger baseline than a static initial prompt \cite{li2024cbwhisper}. The third is a controlled streaming study varying endpointing delay, chunk size and model size while jointly reporting WER, median/p95 end-to-end latency and cold start. Repeated RTF trials should replace single observations.

For adaptation, synthetic domain speech \cite{tran2025das}, text-only fine-tuning \cite{fang2025textonly} and decoupled two-stage adaptation \cite{zheng2026twostage} fit the privacy constraint because written minutes and glossaries may be available when meeting audio is not. Before real-user deployment, authentication, per-user document isolation, upload limits and retention rules are mandatory. Finally, accessibility effectiveness belongs to Topic~3 and requires participatory evaluation with deaf or hard-of-hearing users; it cannot be inferred from WER alone.
